CREATED:20251007T015211Z
DESCRIPTION:The Duke Turkish Program & Screen/Society present:\n\n"Şerafet
 tin Can Atalay"\n(Nebil Özgentürk\, 2025\, Turkish with English subtitles
 \, Digital)\n\nThis documentary film portrays the life and public struggl
 e of Can Atalay\, a prominent Turkish human rights lawyer and and elected
  Member of Parliament who remains imprisoned despite a binding ruling by 
 Turkey's Constitutional Court-revealing a profound constitutional and dem
 ocratic crisis.\n\nFor decades\, Atalay has represented victims of state 
 violence\, labor disasters\, environmental destruction\, and political re
 pression. Through interviews\, archival footage\, and personal reflection
 s\, the film traces Atalay's legal advocacy on behalf of workers\, enviro
 nmental activists\, and civil society groups\, as well as the broader dem
 ocratic and judicial challenges in contemporary Turkey. Framed as both a 
 personal portrait and a political chronicle\, the film explores themes of
  justice\, resistance\, solidarity\, and the costs of dissent from the Ge
 zi Park protests of 2013 to the present.\n\nThe screening will include sp
 ecial participation by acclaimed filmmaker Nebil Özgentürk\, offering aud
 iences the opportunity to engage directly with the filmmaker's perspectiv
 e on the story and its wider implications.\n\n== ABOUT THE FILMMAKER: ==\
 nAs an award-winning journalist\, documentary filmmaker\, and writer\, fo
 r more than thirty years Nebil Özgentürk (link opens in a new window/tab)
  has been one of Turkey's most important chroniclers of cultural and poli
 tical memory\, documenting the lives and ideas of the country's leading w
 riters\, thinkers\, artists\, and public intellectuals. His work has long
  served as a living archive of Turkey's intellectual life.\n\nToday\, his
  camera turns toward a different subject: the erosion of the rule of law 
 itself. Having spent decades preserving collective memory\, Özgentürk now
  documents its deepest injustices.\n\n==\nView trailer: https://www.youtu
 be.com/watch?v=cz-YRRW94pU\n\nParking Info: https://artscenter.duke.edu/p
 arking
